\section{Terms Are Trees with Named Holes}

A compiler tool can represent a tiny expression as \verb|add(reg(r0), imm(1))|. Its root has functor \texttt{add} and arity two. The leaves \texttt{r0} and \texttt{1} are an atom and an integer, respectively; neither is a variable. A term such as \verb|add(reg(R), imm(N))| has two variables and is not ground. The same variable occurring twice in a term is one hole shared between positions. In Prolog the standalone anonymous variable \verb|_| is an exception to spelling-based sharing: each occurrence is fresh. When used as a goal, an outer symbol also has a predicate role, but a compound term by itself does not call anything.

A substitution maps variables to terms. Unification solves a set of term equations by finding one substitution that makes both sides of every equation identical. It is symmetric, unlike a matcher that binds variables on only one side. A \emph{most general unifier} (MGU) leaves anything unconstrained free: every other unifier can be obtained by further instantiating the result. The variable picked to represent an unconstrained equivalence class can differ across implementations. Van Emden and Kowalski use most general unifiers in their account of resolution; unification is the local step that joins a goal with the head of a fresh clause.

\section{Test Equality Without Evaluating a Term}

Run the following at a Prolog top level. The commented lines give representative answers; variable aliasing and cyclic terms can print differently across systems.

\begin{verbatim}
?- inst(Op, reg(R), R) = inst(add, reg(r0), D).
% Op = add, R = D, D = r0 (or R = r0, D = r0).
?- copy(reg(X), reg(X)) = copy(reg(r0), reg(r1)).
% false.
?- imm(1+1) = imm(2).
% false: the left argument is a compound arithmetic expression.
?- unify_with_occurs_check(X, wrap(X)).
% false.
\end{verbatim}

The first goal binds variables on both sides. The second fails after the repeated variable forces \texttt{r0} and \texttt{r1} to agree. The third illustrates that \texttt{=/2} does not evaluate an arithmetic expression. In Prolog, \texttt{is/2} evaluates its right operand first, subject to its numeric and instantiation requirements. Luger and Stubblefield's chapter on unification discusses the interaction of binding, evaluation, and backtracking; those jobs should stay separate in the compiler tool as well.

An MGU need not fill every hole. Unifying \verb|copy(reg(A), B)| with \verb|copy(reg(C), reg(C))| makes \texttt{A} and \texttt{C} aliases and makes \texttt{B} a register term containing that same unbound variable. Binding \texttt{C} to \texttt{r0} would also solve the original equations but would gratuitously exclude \texttt{r1}. A later instruction constraint can still choose between them. This is why a compiler pass must not mistake a successful unification for proof that the output is ground.

