\section{Walkthrough: Keep Equations and Bindings Consistent}

Here is finite-tree unification pseudocode, starting with acyclic inputs and an empty substitution. A worklist equation may contain pointers to previously bound variables. To \emph{walk} a root is to chase variable aliases until the root is unbound or nonvariable.

\begin{verbatim}
pending := input equations; bindings := empty
while pending is not empty:
    take (s, t); walk both roots through bindings
    if s and t are the same variable or same constant: continue
    if only t is a variable: swap s, t
    if s is a variable:
        if occurs(s, t, bindings): fail
        bind s to t; continue         # pending will see this binding
    if s and t have the same compound functor and arity:
        add the argument equations to pending
    else: fail                       # unequal leaves or constructors
return bindings                       # recursively expand if needed
\end{verbatim}

The occurs test walks every visited subterm through current bindings, not just the original syntax. For the first query above, decomposition yields \verb|Op = add|, \verb|reg(R) = reg(r0)|, and \verb|R = D|. Take these left to right, processing the argument equation from \texttt{reg} immediately. After \verb|R = r0|, the last equation walks to \verb|r0 = D| and binds \texttt{D}. The invariant is that solutions of the input equations are exactly the substitutions extending the bindings and solving the pending equations (with aliases interpreted through the bindings). Decomposition preserves that invariant by equality of corresponding arguments; variable binding removes an equation without choosing a value for any unrelated variable. An implementation can normalize bindings on return if the caller needs fully expanded terms. Holm's Scheme implementation provides a contrasting representation in which a substitution is explicit data.

\section{Finite Trees Are Not Rational Trees}

Starting with \verb|X = Y| followed by \verb|Y = wrap(X)|, a shallow textual check for \texttt{Y} inside \verb|wrap(X)| would miss the alias. The correct check follows \texttt{X} back to the same variable class as \texttt{Y}, as in \Cref{fig:visual-339}. No finite tree equals a proper subtree of itself.

