\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Walking the argument exposes a cycle before binding. The dashed edge must not be installed by a finite-tree unifier.}
\label{fig:visual-339}
\end{figure}

With SWI-Prolog's default occurs-check setting, the query \verb|?- X = wrap(X).| succeeds with a cyclic term. It represents the regular infinite tree \(\mathrm{wrap}(\mathrm{wrap}(\cdots))\) by a finite cyclic graph, a \emph{rational tree}. This does not satisfy finite-tree equality. Operations on such terms need a cyclic-aware implementation or a documented restriction to acyclic inputs. SWI's \texttt{unify\_with\_occurs\_check/2} prevents creating new cycles but can accept inputs which were already cyclic. Use \texttt{acyclic\_term/1} checks as well if the interface must reject those inputs. Do not silently borrow unchecked unification for a finite syntax-tree or type representation: the constructor equations would then be solved in the wrong domain.

A compiler pass should also state whether it exports an MGU with residual variables or requires a fully reified, ground term. Those are different output contracts: forcing a register choice while unifying an instruction template can discard legal later allocations.
